{"abstract":"Despite the widespread use of “artificial intelligence” (AI) framing in Natural Language Processing (NLP) research, it is not clear what researchers mean by “intelligence”. To that end, we present the results of a survey on the notion of “intelligence” among researchers and its role in the research agenda. The survey elicited complete responses from 303 researchers from a variety of fields including NLP, Machine Learning (ML), Cognitive Science, Linguistics, and Neuroscience. We identify 3 criteria of intelligence that the community agrees on the most: generalization, adaptability, & reasoning. Our results suggests that the perception of the current NLP systems as “intelligent” is a minority position (29%). Furthermore, only 16.2% of the respondents see developing intelligent systems as a research goal, and these respondents are more likely to consider the current systems intelligent.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Højer, Bertram","Jakobsen, Terne Sasha Thorn","Rogers, Anna","Heinrich, Stefan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-818244"],"event":{"acronym":"ACL","title":"Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics"},"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Adaptability","Generalization","Intelligence","Natural Language Processing","Reasoning"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"research community perspectives on intelligence and large language models","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.20959","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-818244","ids":["itu-7b79a87e-93e1-4692-bd39-33e082c20ecd"],"keyword":["adaptability","generalization","intelligence","natural language processing","reasoning"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-5297661960450E9CB6E306059914D9DB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.20959","version":"Pre-print"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.20959","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NLPnorth"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bertram","key":"højerpau","last":"Højer","locid":"363f336f-ee57-420f-990f-11e8c2f0e2c6","name":"Højer, Bertram","orcid":"0009-0002-2990-8752","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Terne Sasha Thorn","key":"jakobsenpau","last":"Jakobsen","name":"Jakobsen, Terne Sasha Thorn","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anna","key":"rogerspau","last":"Rogers","locid":"c40e1e8a-50ee-4a7c-b7f0-2fc0fdd62adc","name":"Rogers, Anna","orcid":"0000-0002-4845-4023","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Stefan","key":"heinrichpau","last":"Heinrich","locid":"1abda59f-c493-4824-a7b1-7470d66f08d1","name":"Heinrich, Stefan","orcid":"0000-0001-9913-3206","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2505.20959","place":"Vienna","publisher":"Association for Computational Linguistics","series":"Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics","status":"p","title":"Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics 2025"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-7b79a87e-93e1-4692-bd39-33e082c20ecd"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Research Community Perspectives on “Intelligence” and Large Language Models","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-818244","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"This paper investigates the challenges of affect control in large language models (LLMs), focusing on their ability to express appropriate emotional states during extended dialogues. We evaluated state-of-the-art open-weight LLMs to assess their affective expressive range in terms of arousal and valence. Our study employs a novel methodology combining LLM-based sentiment analysis with multiturn dialogue simulations between LLMs. We quantify the models' capacity to express a wide spectrum of emotions and how they fluctuate during interactions. Our findings reveal significant variations among LLMs in their ability to maintain consistent affect, with some models demonstrating more stable emotional trajectories than others. Furthermore, we identify key challenges in affect control, including difficulties in producing and maintaining extreme emotional states and limitations in adapting affect to changing conversational contexts. These findings have important implications for the development of more emotionally intelligent AI systems and highlight the need for improved affect modelling in LLMs.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Fazzi, Gino Franco","Skoven Hinge, Julie","Heinrich, Stefan","Burelli, Paolo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-798546"],"filters":{"added":"2025-04","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Affective Computing","Conversational agents","Emotion recognition","Human Computer Interaction HCI","Large Language Models LLMs","Natural Language Processing NLP"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"don t get too excited eliciting emotions in llms","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2503.02457","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-798546","identifier":[{"id":"105005739891","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-8979b659-ccbc-41f4-9027-394f56a3a013"],"keyword":["Affective computing","Conversational agents","Emotion in AI","Emotion recognition","Emottionally intelligent agents","Human-Computer interaction (HCI)","Large Language Models (LLMs)","Natural language processing (NLP)","Trust and engagement in AI","Valence-arousal space"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-FCC06BA1955CB1612C0235F04BB17BFA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2503.02457","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NLPnorth","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, brAIn lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Gino Franco","key":"fazzipau","last":"Fazzi","name":"Fazzi, Gino Franco","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Julie","key":"skovenhingepau","last":"Skoven Hinge","name":"Skoven Hinge, Julie","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Stefan","key":"heinrichpau","last":"Heinrich","locid":"1abda59f-c493-4824-a7b1-7470d66f08d1","name":"Heinrich, Stefan","orcid":"0000-0001-9913-3206","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"cf5e1d64-5489-4534-ba34-8dba5fd354a6","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2503.02457","eisbn":["{9798400713958/2025/04"],"publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 2025 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-8979b659-ccbc-41f4-9027-394f56a3a013"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Don’t Get Too Excited - Eliciting Emotions in LLMs","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-798546","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Neural decoding is an important method in cognitive neuroscience that aims to decode brain representations from recorded neural activity using a multivariate machine learning model. The THINGS initiative provides a large EEG dataset of 46 subjects watching rapidly shown images. Here, we test the feasibility of using this method for decoding high-level object features using recent deep learning models. We create a derivative dataset from this of living vs non-living entities test 15 different deep learning models with 5 different architectures and compare to a SOTA linear model. We show that the linear model is not able to solve the decoding task, while almost all the deep learning models are successful, suggesting that in some cases non-linear models are needed to decode neural representations. We also run a comparative study of the models’ performance on individual object categories, and suggest how artificial neural networks can be used to study brain activity.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Dixen, Laurits","Heinrich, Stefan","Burelli, Paolo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-798535"],"filters":{"added":"2025-04","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Benchmark","Deep learning","EEG","Neural decoding","Things"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"exploring deep learning models for eeg neural decoding","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105000980847","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-798535","identifier":[{"id":"105000980847","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-65008d59-2725-4f72-af0d-65781c75a91a"],"keyword":["Benchmark","Deep Learning","EEG","Neural Decoding","THINGS"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-542395422C230DF5EB64C8848F0D378D"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Creative AI Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, brAIn lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Laurits","key":"dixenpau","last":"Dixen","locid":"e20164d2-7e0c-4f05-88be-ca477dfc5f8c","name":"Dixen, Laurits","orcid":"0009-0001-4447-5222","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Stefan","key":"heinrichpau","last":"Heinrich","locid":"1abda59f-c493-4824-a7b1-7470d66f08d1","name":"Heinrich, Stefan","orcid":"0000-0001-9913-3206","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"cf5e1d64-5489-4534-ba34-8dba5fd354a6","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-031-82487-6_12","isbn":["9783031824869"],"issn":["03029743"],"pages":"162-175","publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","title":"International Symposium on Artificial Intelligence and Neuroscience"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105000980847"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-65008d59-2725-4f72-af0d-65781c75a91a"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Exploring Deep Learning Models for EEG Neural Decoding","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-798535","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Recent advancements in large language models (LLMs) have resulted in increasingly anthropomorphic language concerning the ability of LLMs to reason. Whether reasoning in LLMs should be understood to be inherently different is, however, widely debated. We propose utilizing a representation engineering approach wherein model activations are read from the residual stream of an LLM when processing a reasoning task. The activations are used to derive a control vector that is applied to the model as an inference-time intervention, modulating the representational space of the model, to improve performance on the specified task. We publish the code for deriving control vectors and analyzing model representations. The method allows us to improve performance on reasoning benchmarks and assess how control vectors influence the final logit distribution of a model via metrics such as KL divergence and entropy. We apply control vectors to Mistral-7B-Instruct and a range of Pythia models on an inductive, a deductive and mathematical reasoning task. We show that an LLM can, to a certain degree, be controlled to improve its perceived reasoning ability by modulating activations. The intervention is dependent upon the ability to reliably extract the model's typical state when correctly solving a task. Our results suggest that reasoning performance can be modulated in the same manner as other information-processing tasks performed by LLMs and demonstrate that we are capable of improving performance on specific tasks via a simple intervention on the residual stream with no additional training.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Højer, Bertram","Jarvis, Oliver","Heinrich, Stefan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-818230"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Intelligence","Large Language Models","Reasoning","Representation learning"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"improving reasoning performance in large language models via representation engineering","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=IssPhpUsKt","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-818230","identifier":[{"id":"105010216683","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-03c0f5a1-235b-470a-9637-45630c03c4a3"],"keyword":["Intelligence","Large Language Models","Reasoning","Representation Learning"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-62E5D0910088A8E1E22249FA426ADC88"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=IssPhpUsKt","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.19483","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NLPnorth"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bertram","key":"højerpau","last":"Højer","locid":"363f336f-ee57-420f-990f-11e8c2f0e2c6","name":"Højer, Bertram","orcid":"0009-0002-2990-8752","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"jarvispau","last":"Jarvis","name":"Jarvis, Oliver","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Stefan","key":"heinrichpau","last":"Heinrich","locid":"1abda59f-c493-4824-a7b1-7470d66f08d1","name":"Heinrich, Stefan","orcid":"0000-0001-9913-3206","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["9798331320850"],"pages":"1-18","place":"Singapore","status":"p","title":"13th International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR 2025)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105010216683"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-03c0f5a1-235b-470a-9637-45630c03c4a3"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Improving Reasoning Performance in Large Language Models via Representation Engineering","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-818230","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Experiments on ASDEX Upgrade (AUG) in 2021 and 2022 have addressed a number of critical issues for ITER and EU DEMO. A major objective of the AUG programme is to shed light on the underlying physics of confinement, stability, and plasma exhaust in order to allow reliable extrapolation of results obtained on present day machines to these reactor-grade devices. Concerning pedestal physics, the mitigation of edge localised modes (ELMs) using resonant magnetic perturbations (RMPs) was found to be consistent with a reduction of the linear peeling-ballooning stability threshold due to the helical deformation of the plasma. Conversely, ELM suppression by RMPs is ascribed to an increased pedestal transport that keeps the plasma away from this boundary. Candidates for this increased transport are locally enhanced turbulence and a locked magnetic island in the pedestal. The enhanced D-alpha (EDA) and quasi-continuous exhaust (QCE) regimes have been established as promising ELM-free scenarios. Here, the pressure gradient at the foot of the H-mode pedestal is reduced by a quasi-coherent mode, consistent with violation of the high-n ballooning mode stability limit there. This is suggestive that the EDA and QCE regimes have a common underlying physics origin. In the area of transport physics, full radius models for both L- and H-modes have been developed. These models predict energy confinement in AUG better than the commonly used global scaling laws, representing a large step towards the goal of predictive capability. A new momentum transport analysis framework has been developed that provides access to the intrinsic torque in the plasma core. In the field of exhaust, the X-Point Radiator (XPR), a cold and dense plasma region on closed flux surfaces close to the X-point, was described by an analytical model that provides an understanding of its formation as well as its stability, i.e., the conditions under which it transitions into a deleterious MARFE with the potential to result in a disruptive termination. With the XPR close to the divertor target, a new detached divertor concept, the compact radiative divertor, was developed. Here, the exhaust power is radiated before reaching the target, allowing close proximity of the X-point to the target. No limitations by the shallow field line angle due to the large flux expansion were observed, and sufficient compression of neutral density was demonstrated. With respect to the pumping of non-recycling impurities, the divertor enrichment was found to mainly depend on the ionisation energy of the impurity under consideration. In the area of MHD physics, analysis of the hot plasma core motion in sawtooth crashes showed good agreement with nonlinear 2-fluid simulations. This indicates that the fast reconnection observed in these events is adequately described including the pressure gradient and the electron inertia in the parallel Ohm’s law. Concerning disruption physics, a shattered pellet injection system was installed in collaboration with the ITER International Organisation. Thanks to the ability to vary the shard size distribution independently of the injection velocity, as well as its impurity admixture, it was possible to tailor the current quench rate, which is an important requirement for future large devices such as ITER. Progress was also made modelling the force reduction of VDEs induced by massive gas injection on AUG. The H-mode density limit was characterised in terms of safe operational space with a newly developed active feedback control method that allowed the stability boundary to be probed several times within a single discharge without inducing a disruptive termination. Regarding integrated operation scenarios, the role of density peaking in the confinement of the ITER baseline scenario (high plasma current) was clarified. The usual energy confinement scaling ITER98(p,y) does not capture this effect, but the more recent H20 scaling does, highlighting again the importance of developing adequate physics based models. Advanced tokamak scenarios, aiming at large non-inductive current fraction due to non-standard profiles of the safety factor in combination with high normalised plasma pressure were studied with a focus on their access conditions. A method to guide the approach of the targeted safety factor profiles was developed, and the conditions for achieving good confinement were clarified. Based on this, two types of advanced scenarios (‘hybrid’ and ‘elevated’ q-profile) were established on AUG and characterised concerning their plasma performance.","author_count":430,"contributors":["Zohm, H.","Alessi, E.","Angioni, C.","Arden, N.","Artigues, V.","Astrain, M.","Asunta, O.","Balden, M.","Bandaru, V.","Banon Navarro, A.","Bauer, M.","Bergmann, A.","Bergmann, M.","Bernardo, J.","Bernert, M.","Biancalani, A.","Bielajew, R.","Bilato, R.","Birkenmeier, G.","Blanken, T.","Bobkov, V.","Bock, A.","Bock, L.","Body, T.","Bolzonella, T.","Bonanomi, N.","Bortolon, A.","Böswirth, B.","Bottereau, C.","Bottino, A.","van den Brand, H.","Brenzke, M.","Brezinsek, S.","Brida, D.","Brochard, F.","Buchanan, J.","Buhler, A.","Burckhart, A.","Camenen, Y.","Cannas, B.","Cano Megías, P.","Carlton, D.","Carr, M.","Carvalho, P.","Castaldo, C.","Castillo Castillo, A.","Cathey, A.","Cavedon, M.","Cazzaniga, C.","Challis, C.","Chankin, A.","Chomiczewska, A.","Cianfarani, C.","Clairet, F.","Coda, S.","Coelho, R.","Coenen, J.W.","Colas, L.","Conway, G.","Costea, S.","Coster, D.","Cote, T.","Creely, A.J.","Croci, G.","Cruz Zabala, D.J.","Cseh, G.","Cziegler, I.","D’Arcangelo, O.","Dal Molin, A.","David, P.","Day, C.","de Baar, M.","de Marné, P.","Delogu, R.","Denner, P.","Di Siena, A.","Dibon, M.","Dominguez-Palacios Durán, J.J.","Dunai, D.","Dreval, M.","Dunne, M.","Duval, B.P.","Dux, R.","Eich, T.","Elgeti, S.","Encheva, A.","Esposito, B.","Fable, E.","Faitsch, M.","Fajardo Jimenez, D.","Fantz, U.","Farnik, M.","Faugel, H.","Felici, F.","Ficker, O.","Figuere