f Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","Other Hospitals","Other Research","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark","private practice ølsemaglevej"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-b541ba10-d828-4066-a572-44606a355a1c","ku-9d1945fe-0b09-4195-93f8-cd839304b846","nfa-1ed6a668-cf82-4f8c-8fa8-6a85a426195c","sdu-3711c9b5-23ce-412e-ba33-e4a257373b36"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen","NFA National Research Centre for the Working Environment","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Meta-analysis of ACE inhibitor–induced angioedema identifies novel risk locus","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S04-091.21-767798","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Reinforcement learning policies are typically represented by black-box neural networks, which are non-interpretable and not well-suited for safety-critical domains. To address both of these issues, we propose constrained normalizing flow policies as interpretable and safe-by-construction policy models. We achieve safety for reinforcement learning problems with instantaneous safety constraints, for which we can exploit domain knowledge by analytically constructing a normalizing flow that ensures constraint satisfaction. The normalizing flow corresponds to an interpretable sequence of transformations on action samples, each ensuring alignment with respect to a particular constraint. Our experiments reveal benefits beyond interpretability in an easier learning objective and maintained constraint satisfaction throughout the entire learning process. Our approach leverages constraints over reward engineering while offering enhanced interpretability, safety, and direct means of providing domain knowledge to the agent without relying on complex reward functions.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Rietz, Finn","Schaffernicht, Erik","Heinrich, Stefan","Stork, Johannes A."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776375"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Örebro University"],"keyword":["Interpretability","Normalizing flows","Reinforcement learning","Safety"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"towards interpretable reinforcement learning with constrained normalizing flow policies","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2405.01198","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-776375","ids":["itu-1a09103b-e8e1-4b48-8429-6924533d0998"],"keyword":["Constraint-based policies","Interpretability","Normalizing flows","Reinforcement learning","Safety"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-86D5D61B0216F2AFF0EE7D49D3291E2D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"rem","url":"https://harlworkshop.github.io/2024/submission_1.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2405.01198","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"se","key":"örebro university","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Örebro University","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NLPnorth","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, brAIn lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Finn","key":"rietzpau","last":"Rietz","name":"Rietz, Finn","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Erik","key":"schaffernichtpau","last":"Schaffernicht","name":"Schaffernicht, Erik","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Stefan","key":"heinrichpau","last":"Heinrich","locid":"1abda59f-c493-4824-a7b1-7470d66f08d1","name":"Heinrich, Stefan","orcid":"0000-0001-9913-3206","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Johannes A.","key":"storkpau","last":"Stork","name":"Stork, Johannes A.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2405.01198","place":"Vienna, AT","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the ICRA2024 Workshop on Human-aligned Reinforcement Learning for Autonomous Agents and Robots"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-1a09103b-e8e1-4b48-8429-6924533d0998"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Towards Interpretable Reinforcement Learning with Constrained Normalizing Flow Policies","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776375","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"In 2021 JET exploited its unique capabilities to operate with T and D–T fuel with an ITER-like Be/W wall (JET-ILW). This second major JET D–T campaign (DTE2), after DTE1 in 1997, represented the culmination of a series of JET enhancements—new fusion diagnostics, new T injection capabilities, refurbishment of the T plant, increased auxiliary heating, in-vessel calibration of 14 MeV neutron yield monitors—as well as significant advances in plasma theory and modelling in the fusion community. DTE2 was complemented by a sequence of isotope physics campaigns encompassing operation in pure tritium at high T-NBI power. Carefully conducted for safe operation with tritium, the new T and D–T experiments used 1 kg of T (vs 100 g in DTE1), yielding the most fusion reactor relevant D–T plasmas to date and expanding our understanding of isotopes and D–T mixture physics. Furthermore, since the JET T and DTE2 campaigns occurred almost 25 years after the last major D–T tokamak experiment, it was also a strategic goal of the European fusion programme to refresh operational experience of a nuclear tokamak to prepare staff for ITER operation. The key physics results of the JET T and DTE2 experiments, carried out within the EUROfusion JET1 work package, are reported in this paper. Progress in the technological exploitation of JET D–T operations, development and validation of nuclear codes, neutronic tools and techniques for ITER operations carried out by EUROfusion (started within the Horizon 2020 Framework Programme and continuing under the Horizon Europe FP) are reported in (Litaudon et al Nucl. Fusion accepted), while JET experience on T and D–T operations is presented in (King et al Nucl. Fusion submitted).","author_count":1075,"contributors":["Maggi, C. F.","Abate, D.","Abid, N.","Abreu, P.","Adabonyan, O.","Afzal, M.","Ahmad, I.","Akhtar, M.","Albanese, R.","Aleiferis, S.","Alessi, E.","Aleynikov, P.","Aleynikov, P.","Alguacil, J.","Alhage, J.","Ali, M.","Allen, H.","