sponding":null,"first":"S.","key":"zoletnikspau","last":"Zoletnik","name":"Zoletnik, S.","rank":410,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1088/1741-4326/ad2f4d","issn":["00295515","10185577"],"issn_org":["00295515"],"issue":"11","journal":"Nuclear Fusion","paperid":"112002","publisher":"IOP Publishing","status":"p","title":"Nuclear Fusion","volume":"64"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85196317947"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-f40dcf0c-fbb8-4061-b51d-f86401abf028"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Overview of the first Wendelstein 7-X long pulse campaign with fully water-cooled plasma facing components","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-789488","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Since minimally invasive liver surgery has proven benefits over open surgery, this technique should also be implemented more broadly in Germany. With the dramatic development in minimally invasive and robotic liver surgery, this approach has been established in recent years. Most recent analyses suggest lower complication rates, blood loss and hospital stay compared to open and laparoscopic liver surgery. In contrast to laparoscopic surgery, the technical setting of robotic liver surgery is widely independent of the type of resection. The laparoscopic and robotic technologies should be considered to be equal at the moment, although most recent analyses even suggest additional advantages of robotic over laparoscopic liver surgery. Moreover, robotics has a greater potential for technical refinements, including the inclusion of artificial intelligence and machine learning. Most steps can be transferred from open and laparoscopic liver surgery, but a dissection device such as the CUSA has not yet been developed. Consequently, different techniques have been reported for parenchymal transsection. Due to the special technical features of robotic surgery, intensive training programs should be used prior to the establishment of a robotic liver surgery program.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Heinrich, Stefan","Tschuor, Christoph","Lang, Hauke"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-701560"],"filters":{"added":"2023-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Mainz University Hospital"],"keyword":["Liver surgery","Minimal invasive surgery","Robotic surgery"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"robotik in der leberchirurgie tipps und tricks","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"djr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-701560","identifier":[{"id":"85167842445","type":"scopus"},{"id":"37130543","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-3cee7720-05bc-43e1-913a-53521cf1542a"],"keyword":["Liver surgery","minimal-invasive surgery","robotic surgery"],"lang":"German","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-51AEABB3C476516507D7E910705BD7AA"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"mainz university hospital","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Mainz University Hospital","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Stefan","key":"heinrichpau","last":"Heinrich","name":"Heinrich, Stefan","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","first":"Christoph","key":"tschuorpau","last":"Tschuor","locid":"8cab259c-03b7-41a7-8c61-22bb94d2a1a6","name":"Tschuor, Christoph","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Hauke","key":"langpau","last":"Lang","name":"Lang, Hauke","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["37130543"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1055/a-2060-9814","issn":["0044409X","14389592"],"issn_org":["0044409X"],"issue":"4","journal":"Zentralblatt fur Chirurgie - Zeitschrift fur Allgemeine, Viszeral- und Gefasschirurgie","pages":"359-366","status":"p","title":"Zentralblatt fur Chirurgie - Zeitschrift fur Allgemeine, Viszeral- und Gefasschirurgie","volume":"148"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85167842445"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-3cee7720-05bc-43e1-913a-53521cf1542a"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Robotik in der Leberchirurgie - Tipps und Tricks","type":"Journal Review","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-701560","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"The objective of this article is to provide a new approach for walking speed learning and generalization in the speed-adaptation control of exoskeletons. By combining the gated and adaptive continuous-time recurrent neural network (GACTRNN), which has the potential to process periodic signals, and the “sequence to sequence” structure, the S2S-GACTRNN model is proposed for walking gait generation at different speeds. The “proactive loop” and “reactive loop” were presented for learning and generalization capability evaluation, respectively, in the experiments of sinusoidal signals and walking gait signals. First, simulation experiments were carried out to evaluate the S2S-GACTRNN's learning and generalization capabilities of sinusoidal signals with different frequencies. Second, in offline experiments, the model was applied to deal with walking gait signals to evaluate the capabilities of learning and generalization of walking speeds. Third, a client-server system was constructed and an online prediction method was proposed for online experiments to further evaluate the trained model's performance in walking speed generalization. The mean absolute errors of S2S-GACTRNN trained using walking data at three speeds were reduced by 24%, 38%, and 24% compared with that trained using walking data at one speed. The results show that the model has learning and generalization potential for gait signals of different walking speeds and may be utilized as a new approach to the adaptive control of walking speeds in the field of exoskeletons.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Zhang, Wuxiang","Ling, Zhitao","Heinrich, Stefan","Ding, Xilun","Feng, Yanggang"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776445"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East"],"country":["China"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Beihang University"],"keyword":["Recurrent neural networks","hip exoskeleton"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"walking speed learning and generalization using seq000000000002seq gated and adaptive continuous time recurrent neural network s000000000002s gactrnn for a hip exoskeleton","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-776445","identifier":[{"id":"85147278304","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-bb7d209c-5a51-4ecd-8570-6f5b82c31397"],"keyword":["hip exoskeleton","learning and generalization","recurrent neural networks"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-E9DB9F6E79C29DD25A9C83456580493A"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"cn","key":"beihang university","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Beihang University","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NLPnorth"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Wuxiang","key":"zhangpau","last":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wuxiang","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Zhitao","key":"lingpau","last":"Ling","name":"Ling, Zhitao","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Stefan","key":"heinrichpau","last":"Heinrich","locid":"1abda59f-c493-4824-a7b1-7470d66f08d1","name":"Heinrich, Stefan","orcid":"0000-0001-9913-3206","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Xilun","key":"dingpau","last":"Ding","name":"Ding, Xilun","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Yanggang","key":"fengpau","last":"Feng","name":"Feng, Yanggang","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"X"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Undefined"]},"sid":["itu-bb7d209c-5a51-4ecd-8570-6f5b82c31397"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Walking Speed Learning and Generalization Using Seq2Seq Gated and Adaptive Continuous-Time Recurrent Neural Network (S2S-GACTRNN) for a Hip Exoskeleton","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776445","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Furigana are pronunciation notes used in Japanese writing. Being able to detect these can help improve optical character recognition (OCR) performance or make more accurate digital copies of Japanese written media by correctly displaying furigana. This project focuses on detecting furigana in Japanese books and comics. While there has been research into the detection of Japanese text in general, there are currently no proposed methods for detecting furigana. We construct a new dataset containing Japanese written media and annotations of furigana. We propose an evaluation metric for such data which is similar to the evaluation protocols used in object detection except that it allows groups of objects to be labeled by one annotation. We propose a method for detection of furigana that is based on mathematical morphology and connected component analysis. We evaluate the detections of the dataset and compare different methods for text extraction. We also evaluate different types of images such as books and comics individually and discuss the challenges of each type of image. The proposed method reaches an F1-score of 76 but less so on comics, and books of irregular format. Finally, we show that the proposed method can improve the performance of OCR by 509 dataset. Source code is available via https://github.com/nikolajkb/FuriganaDetection.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Bjerregaard, Nikolaj Kjøller","Cheplygina, Veronika","Heinrich, Stefan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776393"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"detection of furigana text in images","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/ARXIV.2207.03960","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-776393","ids":["itu-3ea91d3f-5b5a-4e9e-b725-ee258ae5808f"],"keyword":["Connected component analysis","Furigana annotation dataset","Furigana detection","Morphological image processing","OCR for Japanese media"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-B4AF18393F57061343A2C68D42D0ACE8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/ARXIV.2207.03960","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.03960","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Data-intensive Systems and Applications","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Pattern Recognition Revisited lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Nikolaj Kjøller","key":"bjerregaardpau","last":"Bjerregaard","name":"Bjerregaard, Nikolaj Kjøller","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Veronika","key":"cheplyginapau","last":"Cheplygina","locid":"1c4bfd79-4336-4b17-a672-9acb465f15c0","name":"Cheplygina, Veronika","orcid":"0000-0003-0176-9324","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Stefan","key":"heinrichpau","last":"Heinrich","locid":"1abda59f-c493-4824-a7b1-7470d66f08d1","name":"Heinrich, Stefan","orcid":"0000-0001-9913-3206","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-3ea91d3f-5b5a-4e9e-b725-ee258ae5808f"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Detection of Furigana Text in Images","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776393","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Generative adversarial networks conditioned on textual image descriptions are capable of generating realistic-looking images. However, current methods still struggle to generate images based on complex image captions from a heterogeneous domain. Furthermore, quantitatively evaluating these text-to-image models is challenging, as most evaluation metrics only judge image quality but not the conformity between the image and its caption. To address these challenges we introduce a new model that explicitly models individual objects within an image and a new evaluation metric called Semantic Object Accuracy (SOA) that specifically evaluates images given an image caption. The SOA uses a pre-trained object detector to evaluate if a generated image contains objects that are mentioned in the image caption, e.g., whether an image generated from “a car driving down the street” contains a car. We perform a user study comparing several text-to-image models and show that our SOA metric ranks the models the same way as humans, whereas other metrics such as the Inception Score do not. Our evaluation also shows that models which explicitly model objects outperform models which only model global image characteristics.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Hinz, Tobias","Heinrich, Stefan","Wermter, Stefan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-718914"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany","United States"],"glorg":["Adobe Research","All Global Affiliations","University of Hamburg"],"keyword":["Generative models","evaluation of generative models","generative adversarial network GAN","text to image synthesis"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"semantic object accuracy for generative text to image synthesis","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TPAMI.2020.3021209","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-718914","identifier":[{"id":"85124051702","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-c1e838f2-29d4-4421-871d-ae0015a06cf7"],"keyword":["evaluation of generative models","generative adversarial network (GAN)","generative models","text-to-image synthesis"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-D550E64C42DF800657411DADD9C5CBC1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TPAMI.2020.3021209","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102753063/Semantic_Object_Accuracy_for_Generative_Text-to-Image_Synthesis.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/stamp/stamp.jsp?tp=&arnumber=9184960","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"adobe research","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Adobe Research","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NLPnorth"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"university of hamburg","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Hamburg","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Tobias","key":"hinzpau","last":"Hinz","name":"Hinz, Tobias","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Stefan","key":"heinrichpau","last":"Heinrich","locid":"1abda59f-c493-4824-a7b1-7470d66f08d1","name":"Heinrich, Stefan","orcid":"0000-0001-9913-3206","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Stefan","key":"wermterpau","last":"Wermter","name":"Wermter, Stefan","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/tpami.2020.3021209","issn":["01628828"],"journal":"IEEE Transactions on Pattern Analysis and Machine Intelligence","pages":"1552-1565","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"IEEE Transactions on Pattern Analysis and Machine Intelligence","volume":"44"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-c1e838f2-29d4-4421-871d-ae0015a06cf7"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Semantic Object Accuracy for Generative Text-to-Image Synthesis","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-718914","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"In 2008 we published the first set of guidelines for standardizing research in autophagy. Since then, research on this topic has continued to accelerate, and many new scientists have entered the field. Our knowledge base and relevant new technologies have also been expanding. Accordingly, it is important to update these guidelines formonitoring autophagy in different organisms. Various reviews have described the range of assays that have been used for this purpose. Nevertheless, there continues to be confusion regarding acceptable methods to measure autophagy, especially in multicellular eukaryotes. A key point that needs to be emphasized is that there is a Do not distribute. difference between measurements that monitor the numbers or volume of autophagic elements (e.g., autophagosomes or autolysosomes) at any stage of the autophagic process vs. those that measure flux through the autophagy pathway (i.e., the complete process); thus, a block in macroautophagy that results in autophagosome accumulation needs to be differentiated fromstimuli that result in increased autophagic activity, defined as increased autophagy induction coupled with increased delivery to, and degradation within, lysosomes (in most higher eukaryotes and some protists such as Dictyostelium) or the vacuole (in plants and fungi). In other words, it is especially important that investigators new to the field understand that the appearance of more autophagosomes does not necessarily equate withmore autophagy. In fact, in many cases, autophagosomes accumulate because of a block in trafficking to lysosomes without a concomitant change in autophagosome biogenesis,whereas an increase in autolysosomes may reflect a reduction in degradative activity. Here, we present a set of guidelines for the selection and interpretation of methods for use by investigators who aim to examine macroautophagy and related processes, as well as for reviewers who need to provide realistic and reasonable critiques of papers that are focused on these processes. These guidelines are not meant to be a formulaic set of rules, because the appropriate assays depend in part on the question being asked and the system being used. In addition, we emphasize that no individual assay is guaranteed to be themost appropriate one in every situation, and we strongly recommend the use of multiple assays to monitor autophagy. In these guidelines, we consider these various methods of assessing autophagy and what information can, or cannot, be obtained from them. Finally, by discussing the merits and limits of particular autophagy assays, we hope to encourage technical innovation in the field.","author_count":2922,"contributors":["Klionsky, Daniel J","Abdel-Aziz, Amal Kamal","Abdelfatah, Sara","Abdellatif, Mahmoud","Abdoli, Asghar","Abel, Steffen","Abeliovich, Hagai","Abildgaard, Marie H","Abudu, Yakubu Princely","Acevedo-Arozena, Abraham","Adamopoulos, Iannis E","Adeli, Khosrow","Adolph, Timon E","Adornetto, Annagrazia","Aflaki, Elma","Agam, Galila","Agarwal, Anupam","Aggarwal, Bharat B","Agnello, Maria","Agostinis, Patrizia","Agrewala, Javed N","Agrotis, Alexander","Aguilar, Patricia V","Ahmad, S Tariq","Ahmed, Zubair M","Ahumada-Castro, Ulises","Aits, Sonja","Aizawa, Shu","Akkoc, Yunus","Akoumianaki, Tonia","Akpinar, Hafize Aysin","Al-Abd, Ahmed M","Al-Akra, Lina","Al-Gharaibeh, Abeer","Alaoui-Jamali, Moulay A","Alberti, Simon","Alcocer-Gómez, Elísabet","Alessandri, Cristiano","Ali, Muhammad","Alim Al-Bari, M Abdul","Aliwaini, Saeb","Alizadeh, Javad","Almacellas, Eugènia","Almasan, Alexandru","Alonso, Alicia","Alonso, Guillermo D","Altan-Bonnet, Nihal","Altieri, Dario C","Álvarez, Élida M C","Alves, Sara","Alves da Costa, Cristine","Alzaharna, Mazen M","Amadio, Marialaura","Amantini, Consuelo","Amaral, Cristina","Ambrosio, Susanna","Amer, Amal O","Ammanathan, Veena","An, Zhenyi","Andersen, Stig U","Andrabi, Shaida A","Andrade-Silva, Magaiver","Andres, Allen M","Angelini, Sabrina","Ann, David",